Requirements

Logging Support for Multiple Levels:
The system must support logging at different levels such as INFO, DEBUG, and ERROR.

Multiple Output Sinks:
Logs can be sent to various outputs such as console, file, and database.

Dynamic Configuration:
The application should allow adding or removing logging sinks dynamically for specific log levels.

Prevent Duplicate Sink Registrations:
The same sink should not be registered more than once for the same log level.

Singleton Logger Instance:
There should be only one global logger instance shared across the application.

Simple API for Logging:
Easy-to-use methods such as info(), debug(), and error() for client code.


Entities

LogLevel (Enum):
Represents the severity of the logs — INFO, DEBUG, and ERROR.

SinkType (Enum):
Represents the type of output sink — CONSOLE, FILE, DATABASE.

LogObserver (Interface):
Observer interface for different sinks. Each sink implements a log(String message) method.

ConsoleLogger, FileLogger, DataBaseLogger (Concrete Observers):
Implementations of LogObserver for respective sinks.

LogSinkSubject (Subject):
Manages the observers (sinks) for each log level. Notifies appropriate sinks when a log event happens.

LogManager:
Helps with creating observers/sinks and adding them to LogSinkSubject.

Logger (Singleton):
Central logging class, responsible for formatting and sending logs to the right sinks based on the log level.



Design Patterns Used and Why
1. Singleton Pattern (Logger class)

Why:
Ensures there is only one instance of the logger throughout the application lifecycle.
Provides a global access point to the logger instance.
Thread-safe double-checked locking guarantees safe initialization in multi-threaded environments.

2. Observer Pattern (LogSinkSubject and LogObserver)

Why:
Decouples the log event source (Logger) from the log output sinks.
Allows multiple sinks (observers) to register and be notified independently when a log event occurs.
Makes it easy to add or remove sinks dynamically without changing the core logger logic.
Facilitates managing sinks per log level separately.




public enum LogLevel {
    INFO, DEBUG, ERROR
}


public enum SinkType {
    CONSOLE, FILE, DATABASE
}

public interface LogObserver {
    void log(String message);
}

public class ConsoleLogger implements LogObserver {
    @Override
    public void log(String message) {
        System.out.println("Console: " + message);
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object obj) {
        return obj != null && this.getClass() == obj.getClass();
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return this.getClass().hashCode();
    }
}


public class FileLogger implements LogObserver {
    @Override
    public void log(String message) {
        System.out.println("File: " + message);
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object obj) {
        return obj != null && this.getClass() == obj.getClass();
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return this.getClass().hashCode();
    }
}


public class DataBaseLogger implements LogObserver {
    @Override
    public void log(String message) {
        System.out.println("Database: " + message);
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object obj) {
        return obj != null && this.getClass() == obj.getClass();
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return this.getClass().hashCode();
    }
}


import java.util.*;

public class LogSinkSubject {
    private final Map<LogLevel, List<LogObserver>> observers = new HashMap<>();

    public void addObserver(LogLevel level, LogObserver logObserver) {
        List<LogObserver> logObservers = observers.getOrDefault(level, new ArrayList<>());

        if (!logObservers.contains(logObserver)) {
            logObservers.add(logObserver);
            observers.put(level, logObservers);
            System.out.println("Added observer: " + logObserver.getClass().getSimpleName() + " for level: " + level);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Observer already exists for level: " + level);
        }
    }

    public void notifyObservers(LogLevel level, String message) {
        List<LogObserver> logObservers = observers.get(level);
        if (logObservers != null) {
            for (LogObserver observer : logObservers) {
                observer.log(message);
            }
        }
    }
}


public class LogManager {
    public static LogSinkSubject buildLogSinkSubject(LogLevel logLevel, SinkType sinkType, LogSinkSubject logSinkSubject) {
        switch (sinkType) {
            case CONSOLE -> logSinkSubject.addObserver(logLevel, new ConsoleLogger());
            case FILE -> logSinkSubject.addObserver(logLevel, new FileLogger());
            case DATABASE -> logSinkSubject.addObserver(logLevel, new DataBaseLogger());
            default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid SinkType");
        }
        return logSinkSubject;
    }
}


public class Logger {
    private static volatile Logger loggerInstance;
    private static volatile LogSinkSubject logSinkSubject;

    private Logger() {
        logSinkSubject = new LogSinkSubject();
    }

    public static Logger getLoggerInstance() {
        if (loggerInstance == null) {
            synchronized (Logger.class) {
                if (loggerInstance == null) {
                    loggerInstance = new Logger();
                }
            }
        }
        return loggerInstance;
    }

    public void addLogSinkSubject(LogLevel logLevel, SinkType sinkType) {
        logSinkSubject = LogManager.buildLogSinkSubject(logLevel, sinkType, logSinkSubject);
    }

    private void log(LogLevel level, String message) {
        String formattedMessage = switch (level) {
            case INFO -> "INFO: " + message;
            case DEBUG -> "DEBUG: " + message;
            case ERROR -> "ERROR: " + message;
        };

        logSinkSubject.notifyObservers(level, formattedMessage);
    }

    // Exposed methods for convenience
    public void info(String message) {
        log(LogLevel.INFO, message);
    }

    public void debug(String message) {
        log(LogLevel.DEBUG, message);
    }

    public void error(String message) {
        log(LogLevel.ERROR, message);
    }
}


public class LoggerApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Logger logger = Logger.getLoggerInstance();

        // Add sinks
        logger.addLogSinkSubject(LogLevel.INFO, SinkType.CONSOLE);
        logger.addLogSinkSubject(LogLevel.INFO, SinkType.FILE);
        logger.addLogSinkSubject(LogLevel.ERROR, SinkType.DATABASE);
        logger.addLogSinkSubject(LogLevel.DEBUG, SinkType.CONSOLE);

        // Log messages
        logger.info("Application started.");
        logger.error("An error occurred.");
        logger.debug("Debugging application.");
        
        // Trying to add duplicate sinks (won't add)
        logger.addLogSinkSubject(LogLevel.INFO, SinkType.CONSOLE);
    }
}



WHEN USED BY MULTIPLE APPLICATIONS

Suggested Code-Level Changes

Change Logger to a regular class, instantiated per application:

public class Logger {
    private final LogSinkSubject logSinkSubject;

    public Logger() {
        this.logSinkSubject = new LogSinkSubject();
    }

    // Configuration methods and logging methods remain similar
}


Add a LoggerFactory or LoggerManager to create/get logger instances:

public class LoggerFactory {
    private static final Map<String, Logger> loggers = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public static Logger getLogger(String appId) {
        return loggers.computeIfAbsent(appId, id -> new Logger());
    }
}


Client code obtains logger by appId:

Logger appLogger = LoggerFactory.getLogger("MyApp");
appLogger.addLogSinkSubject(LogLevel.INFO, SinkType.CONSOLE);
appLogger.info("App started.");
